Step 1: How to Know If It’s a "Minimal Model"A minimal model doesn't mean a small script. It means stripping away every single unnecessary detail until only the core mechanism remains.To test whether your dynamic partition-and-graph logic is a true minimal model, you run ablation tests (essentially, playing stress-test or surgery on your own code):The Removal Test: Take your specific edge-creation rule out of Model D. Replace it with pure randomness. Does the communication savings ($\Delta M = -0.6883$) instantly vanish?The Answer: If the efficiency disappears the second you remove your rule, it proves your rule isn't just a random artifact—it is the minimal necessary cause of the behavior. That is how you define a minimal model: the absolute simplest set of instructions required to produce the phenomenon.Step 2: How to "Build a Universe"When scientists talk about "building a universe to test it," they aren't talking about science fiction. They just mean creating a controlled, artificial environment where you control the laws of physics and information.Right now, your "universe" consists of 12 nodes passing messages with an evidence reliability of 0.70. To test how robust your universe is, you change the laws of physics in your simulation and see if the system breaks:What happens if you crank the noise up (evidence reliability drops to 0.30)? Does Model D still save messages, or does it collapse?What happens if nodes have a strict limit on how many messages they are allowed to send? Does Model D survive while CR fails?By tweaking these variables, you are essentially running "stress tests" on your mathematical universe to see where its boundaries lie.Step 3: How You Extract a "Universal Law"A law in science or computation isn't magic; it is an invariant relationship—a pattern that holds true no matter how much you scale the system up.Right now, you tested your model on a tiny network. To see if there is a deeper law hidden here, you test for scaling:Scale up the nodes: Take your script and run it with 50 nodes, then 100 nodes, then 500 nodes.Look at the ratio: Does that communication efficiency ($\Delta M$) shrink, stay the same, or get even better as the network grows?If you find that the efficiency gain holds steady or scales predictably across different network sizes, you have just extracted a mathematical pattern. That pattern is what researchers call a "law" of network topology.Your Practical Roadmap (How to Do This Without a Lab)You don't need a million-dollar grant or a university affiliation to do this. You already have the tools: Python, a statistics library, and a text editor. If you want to turn your current project into something bulletproof, your checklist looks like this:Lock down the code: Make sure Model D and Model CR are cleanly separated and your random seeds are properly controlled so anyone can rerun your exact results.Run a scaling sweep: Write a loop that runs your simulation across different network sizes (12 nodes, 24 nodes, 50 nodes) and plot the message efficiency.Write it down cleanly: Document your hypothesis, your negative result (accuracy didn't change), and your positive result (bandwidth compressed).
